course: ai
lesson: 20
title: In the news: invented court cases and an airline's chatbot
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: When we talk about real news we say "it was reported that", because we were not there and reports can be wrong; these lessons do the same.
explain: It was reported that lawyers in America gave a court a list of past cases to back up their argument, and the cases had been invented by a chatbot. Imagine handing in homework with a list of books from the library, and the librarian finding that not one of them exists.
explain: The lawyers trusted the chatbot because it sounded so sure, and sounding sure is not proof.
explain: Other news showed the same lesson from other sides: a company had to stand behind its chatbot's wrong promise, fake pictures and fake voices hurt real people, and even a big company's picture maker got history wrong.
fact: It was reported that lawyers in America handed a court past cases that a chatbot had invented, and that the judge fined them.
fact: Chatbots can produce made-up cases, books or quotes that sound real; even experts must check them.
fact: Confident writing makes people trust it, but confidence is not proof.
fact: It was reported that more cases of invented court cases in legal papers happened later, in other countries too.
fact: It was reported that an airline's website chatbot told a man he could claim a refund later, which was not the airline's real rule.
fact: It was reported that a tribunal (a kind of court that settles disagreements) decided the airline had to honour what its chatbot had said and did not accept that the bot alone was to blame.
fact: A company is responsible for what its chatbot tells customers.
fact: It was reported that fake, AI-made pictures of a famous singer spread online and upset her fans, and that social media sites struggled to remove them quickly.
fact: Fakes can spread faster than they can be removed; reporting them helps and sharing them harms.
fact: It was reported that a robocall used a fake, AI-made voice of a US president telling people not to vote in an election, and that fines and criminal charges followed.
fact: Using fake voices to trick voters is treated seriously and can be against the law; official sources are the place to check voting rules.
fact: It was reported that a big company's image generator made historically wrong pictures, because its makers' rules were applied even where they did not fit; people criticised it, and the company paused the feature.
exercise: What was reported about the lawyers in America? | They gave a court past cases that a chatbot had invented | They asked a chatbot to be the judge in a case | They used a chatbot to help them win every single case they had | They caught a chatbot stealing real court papers
exercise: Mia asks why the lawyers didn't check. What did Dad say? | They trusted it because it sounded so sure | They had no time because the court was closing | They thought checking was the judge's job | They knew the cases were fake but hoped nobody noticed
exercise: What was reported about what happened to those lawyers? | The judge was not pleased and fined them | The judge thanked them for using new tools | The chatbot was sent to prison instead | Nothing happened because nobody noticed
exercise: Sam hears that fake cases later turned up in court papers in other countries. What is the lesson? | People keep making the same mistake, so learn from it | Only American courts have this problem, so other countries are safe | Fake cases are allowed in some countries | The problem was solved after the first time
exercise: What did the airline's chatbot reportedly tell the man? | That he could claim a refund later, which was not the real rule | That his flight was cancelled when it was really leaving on time | That he had won a free holiday | That his luggage had been lost on purpose
exercise: Jake asks, "Couldn't the airline just say it was the bot's fault?" What was reported? | The tribunal did not accept that, and the airline had to honour the bot's words | The tribunal agreed it was the bot's fault, and the man got nothing back | The bot was taken to court on its own | The airline sued the man for believing the bot
exercise: Who is responsible for what a company's chatbot tells customers? | The company | The chatbot itself | The customer who asked | Nobody at all
exercise: What was reported about the fake pictures of a famous singer? | They were AI-made, spread online and upset her fans | She made them herself to sell more songs | They were real photos stolen from her phone | They were removed before anyone saw them
exercise: Connor's class hears that sites struggled to remove the fake pictures quickly. Why is that a problem? | Fakes spread faster than they can be removed, so millions see them | Sites are not allowed by law to remove any pictures that people post online | The pictures became real after a while | Removing pictures makes them spread faster
exercise: What should you do when you see a fake picture of a real person? | Report it and don't share it | Share it with a warning that it's fake | Save it in case it becomes important | Comment under it to say it looks good
exercise: Chloe's mum mentions a robocall with a fake voice of a US president. What did it tell people? | Not to vote in an election | To vote twice in the same election | To give money to a charity | To stay indoors because of a storm
exercise: Maddie asks if the person behind the robocall was punished. What was reported? | There were fines and charges | There was no punishment of any kind | He was given a prize for clever tricks | The president thanked him for the call
exercise: Where should people check rules about voting? | Official sources, such as the election office | A phone call that sounds like a famous person | A video shared lots of times online | Whatever a chatbot says first
exercise: What was reported about a big company's image generator? | It made historically wrong pictures and the feature was paused | It painted the most accurate history pictures that anyone had ever made | It refused to make any pictures of people | It was only used by history teachers
exercise: Why do we say "it was reported that" about these stories? | We were not there, and reports can be wrong | It makes the story sound more exciting | It means the story is certainly false | It means the story happened long ago
test: "lawyers are smart, if a chatbot gave them cases they must be real" What do you say? | Even experts must check; it was reported the cases were invented | Lawyers can't be wrong about cases, so the cases must have been real | Chatbots only invent things for children | Courts allow made-up cases if they sound good
test: What would have happened if the lawyers had looked up each case first? | They would have found the cases did not exist | The cases would have become real | The judge would have fined them anyway | The chatbot would have been angry with them
test: Your friend says, "If a bot gets it wrong, the company can just blame the bot." Which is closer to what was reported? | A tribunal made the airline honour its chatbot's words | Companies can always blame their bots, so customers get nothing | Bots are sent to court instead of companies | Customers must pay for bots' mistakes
test: "lol just send everyone the fake pic, everyone knows its fake" What is the wise reply? | Don't share it; fakes hurt the real person and spread fast | Share it, fakes don't hurt anyone famous | Share it only with your closest friends | Share it after adding a funny caption
test: In a film, a famous voice on the phone tells a town that voting has moved to next week, and everybody obeys. What should the townspeople have done? | Check the official election office before believing it | Obey, because nobody can ever fake the voice of someone famous | Phone back the same number to ask | Vote twice to be on the safe side
test: Which sentence about these stories is said in the careful news way? | It was reported that a chatbot invented court cases | A chatbot definitely tricked every lawyer in America | Chatbots always invent cases for lawyers | My cousin says chatbots ruin courts
test: "if the chatbot sounds dead sure, it's prob right innit" Best reply? | Sounding sure isn't proof; check it | Yes, bots only sound sure when they're right | Yes, the more sure, the more right | No, bots are always wrong when they're sure
test: Why might a company's image generator get history wrong even though history is clear? | Its makers' rules were applied even where they did not fit | It was trained only on cartoons | It deliberately wanted to fool the children who used it for homework | History books disagree about everything
test: Which of these is a real harm from the fake singer pictures? | The real person was hurt and the fakes stayed online | The singer's songs stopped working | Fans were fined for looking at them | Nothing at all, because the pictures were fake and not really her
test: The man believed the airline's chatbot. Was that silly? | No, people expect a company's website to be right | Yes, nobody should ever believe a website | Yes, he should have asked a different bot | No, because chatbots are never wrong
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reported submission to a court of cases generated by a chatbot? | The cases had been invented, and the advocates had failed to verify them | The cases were genuine but had been incorrectly summarised by the advocates concerned | The court had authorised the use of invented cases | The chatbot had appeared before the court in person
exam: It is given that a tribunal considered a refund promised by an airline's chatbot contrary to the airline's policy. Identify the reported outcome. | The airline was required to honour the representation of its chatbot | The airline was relieved of all responsibility for the chatbot | The customer was required to compensate the airline | The chatbot was held personally liable for the error
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the reported robocall employing a counterfeit presidential voice. | It sought to dissuade citizens from voting and was followed by fines and charges | It encouraged citizens to vote and had been officially approved by the election authorities | It was a genuine recording of the president concerned | It attracted no response from any authority
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which the formula "it was reported that" is employed in respect of news concerning artificial intelligence. | The speaker did not witness the events, and reports may be mistaken | The formula signifies that the report is certainly untrue | The formula is required solely in courts of law | The formula renders a report more dramatic
